## Easy Dot and Polka Nail Designs (Toothpick or Bobby Pin Only)

A dotting tool is not required for this category of <strong>simple nail designs</strong>. It never was, and this is where most simple-nail roundups quietly assume you own one anyway.

**10. Classic polka dots.** Dip a toothpick into polish and press, don't drag, for a clean circle. Vary the pressure slightly and you get natural size variation, which reads as intentional rather than uneven. **11. Graduated dot gradient.** Start with large dots near the cuticle and shrink them progressively toward the tip using the same toothpick. **12. Single statement dot.** One larger dot, made with the rounded end of a bobby pin instead of a toothpick, placed off-centre near the cuticle.

**13. Scattered constellation dots.** Random, uneven placement across a sheer or milky base, deliberately irregular rather than gridded. **14. French dot line.** Instead of a painted French tip, a single row of tiny dots traces the smile line where the tip would normally be. **15. Two-colour dot pairing.** Alternate two dot colours across the nail using the same toothpick, cleaning the tip between colours.

Do I need a dotting tool for polka dot nails? No. A toothpick's tapered point produces small and medium dots with more control than most beginners get from an actual dotting tool, and the rounded head of a bobby pin covers anything larger. The tool matters less than the technique: dip, press straight down, lift straight up, and resist the urge to drag or twist, which is what actually causes smudged, uneven dots. Practise the motion on a paper towel first if your hand shakes; two or three test presses is usually enough to find your pressure.

**16. Ombre dots.** Same graduated sizing as the gradient version, but shift the colour slightly darker toward the tip as the dots shrink. **17. Dotted accent nail only.** Keep nine nails solid and reserve the dot pattern for a single finger, which lowers both time and error risk. **18. Bobby pin swirl.** The flat, rounded end of a bobby pin, dragged lightly rather than pressed, creates a simple swirl or comma shape for anyone ready to move slightly past dots. **19. Negative space dots.** Dots placed only around the edge of an otherwise bare nail, letting the natural nail colour sit at the centre.

