## Negative Space and Minimal Line Designs

An unpainted patch of nail is not a gap you failed to fill in. It is the design.

Negative space technique uses the bare, unpolished nail itself as part of the pattern, letting the natural nail plate stand in for what would otherwise be painted. **28. Cut-out crescent.** A curved, unpainted shape near the cuticle, created by painting around a small piece of tape rather than freehanding the curve. **29. Vertical stripe gap.** A single unpainted line runs the length of the nail, splitting a coloured base into two sections. Tape does the straight-line work here.

**30. Corner triangle.** One unpainted triangular corner near the cuticle, again taped off rather than painted freehand. **31. Checkerboard corner.** Two colours meet at a bare diagonal line for a slightly bolder negative space look, still built entirely from tape guides. **32. Geometric line grid.** Thin painted lines, guided by strips of tape, cross the nail to frame small negative space sections.

Scotch tape is the entire secret to this category. Press a small strip onto the nail before painting, wait until the polish is just past wet, then peel to reveal a crisp, unpainted edge. Byrdie's <a href='https://www.byrdie.com'>minimalist nail roundups</a> return to negative space repeatedly as one of the most reliably editorial-looking techniques a beginner can actually pull off, and the tape method is exactly why. For a deeper set of pattern ideas built entirely on this technique, <a href='/blog/simple-nail-designs/negative-space-nail-ideas'>negative space nail ideas</a> is the dedicated follow-up. Shorter nail beds handle these geometric shapes slightly differently, which <a href='/blog/simple-nail-designs/simple-nail-designs-for-short-nails'>simple nail designs for short nails</a> also addresses directly.

