## Simple Ombre and Gradient Nail Ideas

A makeup sponge does this entire category of <strong>simple nail designs</strong>. Not a brush, not a gradient tool, the same sponge already sitting in your bathroom drawer.

Ombre and gradient technique blends two or more polish shades directly on the nail using a sponge, so the colours melt into each other without a hard line. Paint stripes of each colour directly onto the sponge edge, dab it onto the nail in light, repeated presses, and clean up the skin around the nail once it dries. **33. Classic pink-to-white fade.** The most forgiving version, since both shades are close enough in tone that any blending imperfection barely shows. **34. Sunset gradient.** Warm tones, coral into gold into a soft yellow, blended tip to cuticle for a bolder version of the same technique.

**35. Single-tip ombre.** Just the very tip fades into the base colour rather than the whole nail, a faster, lower-risk version for a first attempt. **36. Reverse ombre.** Colour concentrates at the cuticle and fades toward the tip instead of the other way round. **37. Two-shade jelly gradient.** Sheer, jelly-finish polishes stacked and sponge-blended for a soft-focus fade with more visible depth than solid shades give.

The sponge technique fails almost exclusively for one reason: too much polish loaded onto it at once, which drags rather than dabs. Load lightly, press in small increments, and build the gradient in two or three light passes instead of one heavy one. If your nail beds are shorter, a single-tip ombre or reverse ombre tends to read cleaner than a full-nail fade.

