## How to Make a Simple Nail Design Last Longer

Picture the same manicure at day one and day seven. The difference between those two photos almost never comes down to how complicated the design was.

How long do simple nail designs last? With gel polish and proper prep, a simple manicure lasts 10 to 14 days before noticeable chipping. Regular polish, even on the simplest solid-colour design, typically holds for 4 to 6 days. Complexity of the art has almost no bearing on either number; longevity is a prep and product question, not a design one.

Three things determine how long any of these 40 designs actually holds. First, the cuticle line. Polish that touches skin lifts faster than polish that stops cleanly at the nail plate, so pushing back cuticles before painting matters more than any technique used afterward. Second, the base coat. A ridge-filling base gives polish something to grip; skipping it is the single most common reason a simple design chips within two days. Third, sealing the free edge, running your top coat brush across the very tip of the nail, not just the top surface, on every coat.

None of the 40 designs above need extra durability effort beyond this baseline. A dotted design does not chip faster than a solid colour because it has more polish on it; a negative space design does not last less because part of the nail is bare. The variable is always prep, never pattern.

